From d514cc03ce93129a5e4d18ca083eb3b2cfed2c88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:59:28 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_ui: tell zeo-systray when a task ends out of view

0037's card shows a task ending only to someone looking at the agent panel.
A background task runs for minutes, and the user has usually gone elsewhere
by the time it finishes -- which is when they want to know. zeo-systray, the
tray daemon already fed by Claude Code's hooks, gains a task-outcome datagram
in 0.3.0; this patch is Zeo's side of it (story 013).

When a task ends, the card weighs it once, and sends a datagram only when
both hold:

  * the task was watched (0037's Watch control) or it failed, and
  * its thread is not in front of the user. In view means all four: this
    window is active, this panel's workspace is the one the window displays,
    the panel is open, and the thread is its active one. The workspace check
    matters because multi-workspace windows are on by default, and switching
    a window to another workspace keeps the previous one alive without
    deactivating its panels -- a panel open in a workspace nobody looks at
    would otherwise count as seen, and its endings would never be sent.

The panel answers that question through a query closure it hands the card at
construction, holding the panel weakly and the window by handle: the panel
owns the card, so a handle back would be a cycle. A thread held through two
views delivers each ending twice; the card remembers what it has weighed, so
one ending is one datagram at most, and an ending is news again once its
task leaves the list or runs again -- the rule the thread's own announcements
follow.

The datagram is one JSON object of exactly eight fields, the daemon's
TaskOutcome: task_outcome (completed, failed, stopped or interrupted),
session_id, task_id, cwd, project (the work directory's last component, or
"Zeo" without one), thread_title (always written, null when absent),
task_type and duration_ms. The shape is pinned by one fixture shared byte
for byte with zeo-systray 0.3.0 --
crates/agent_ui/src/tasks_card/test_data/task_outcome.json here,
tests/fixtures/task-outcome.json there -- and both test suites parse it.
Nothing in it can carry a task's description, summary or command line: those
are model-written, and they never leave Zeo.

How it is sent:

  * To $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/zeo-systray.sock and nowhere else. Never a fallback
    such as /tmp: that directory is world-writable, so anyone could bind the
    socket name first and read every outcome Zeo sends. An absent, empty or
    relative XDG_RUNTIME_DIR sends nothing, as the XDG spec asks.
  * From an unbound, non-blocking UnixDatagram, on the background executor.
    A daemon that stopped reading costs a WouldBlock, never a wait; the
    datagram is dropped, and only the session's first failure is logged, at
    debug, with ids only.
  * The thread title is capped at 256 characters, on a char boundary. ACP
    bounds no title, and the daemon reads each datagram into a 64 KiB
    buffer: past it the datagram would arrive cut short, as JSON it cannot
    parse.

zeo-systray is optional at runtime and needs no build-time dependency: with
no daemon running, or no runtime dir, the send is a silent no-op and the card
behaves exactly as in 0037. A daemon older than 0.3.0 cannot parse the
datagram; it logs "discarded malformed datagram" and drops it.

Rides the claude-agent-acp-plus flag BY DEPENDENCY, with 0037: there is no
task to report without the adapter's feed, so it also needs
claude-agent-acp-plus 0.24.0 or later to do anything.

(0.2.3_p20261009): the adapter-supplied type and id are capped like the title, so no field
the agent controls can outgrow the daemon's 64 KiB buffer. An empty or relative
XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is tested as a no-op, and the old "nothing under /tmp" check --
which could not fail -- now asserts no socket path is chosen at all.

Story 064 (2026-11-02): a native subagent row's terminal transition out of
view sends the same datagram, once; datagram_for and should_notify take the
merged TaskRow instead of an AgentTask, so both sources go through one path.
A reopened thread whose child restores an outcome (upstream #66255) announces
no ending: a child whose first observed status is already terminal is history,
not news, while a live Running -> Done still notifies. 10 tests
(tray/subagent_tests.rs, restored_outcome_tests.rs, declared from tray.rs).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---

